<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shi'ism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shiʿism, Shiism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shi'ism is the branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> that holds that leadership of the community belongs to the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> form was the faith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, whose decline ended Ismaili rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> note Shi'ism's influence on the religious landscape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> after the Ilkhanid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oljeitu">Öljeitü</a> converted to it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Buyids also shaped the history of Shia Islam: under their protection Twelver <b>Shiism</b> developed its theology and law in Baghdad, and the public rituals of Ashura and Ghadir took root.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ilkhanate also influenced the religious landscape of Persia: Öljeitü's conversion to Twelver Shiʿism briefly promoted the sect within state structures, an early precedent for the Safavid establishment of <b>Shiʿism</b> as Persia's official religion in 1501.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The conversion of Iran to Twelver <b>Shiism</b> gave the clerical establishment (ulama) a role that expanded significantly from the late seventeenth century, as more activist clerics sought direct influence over appointments, minority communities and public morality, a trend that peaked under Shah Sultan Husayn (r. 1694–1722).</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Shia clerical establishment was treated with considerable respect: Karim Khan supported the construction and upkeep of religious buildings, endowed shrines, and avoided the theological controversies, such as Nader Shah's attempt to have <b>Shi'ism</b> recognised as a fifth Sunni school, that had soured his predecessor's relations with the ulama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1048 al-Mu'izz ibn Badis rejected Fatimid <b>Shiism</b> and recognised the Abbasid caliph, a move welcomed by the Sunni majority.</blockquote><a href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
